<title>CHINA: CHINA IMPORTS 45,000 T NEW ZEALAND WOOL IN 96/97.</title>
<headline>CHINA IMPORTS 45,000 T NEW ZEALAND WOOL IN 96/97.</headline>
<dateline>BEIJING 1997-08-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>China is now the biggest importer of New Zealand wool, importing 45,000 tonnes in 1996/1997, worth about $135 million, the People's Daily Overseas Edition said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>China, including Hong Kong and Taiwan, accounted for 40 percent of New Zealand's international market, the newspaper said but gave no further details.</p>
<p>China imported 67,000 tonnes of New Zealand wool in 1995/96, according to state media.</p>
<p>China consumes about 350,000 tonnes a year, but the country can only supply about 70,000-80,000 tonnes domestically, forcing it to import about 80 percent of demand, it said.</p>
<p>($1 = 8.3 yuan)</p>
<p>-- Beijing Newsroom (86) 10-6532 1921</p>
